Looking back at https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/f3bfd08a118dcb02c1ff9bd3dd6437cb912f1d54/staging/src/k8s.io/api/resource/v1/types.go#L1437, it is not clear whether a name that isn't fully qualified is actually useful. It's defined as "semantically equivalent to a CEL selector with  `device.capacity[<domain>].<name>`", which implies that the `<domain>` part is required.

Looking at https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/f3bfd08a118dcb02c1ff9bd3dd6437cb912f1d54/staging/src/k8s.io/dynamic-resource-allocation/structured/internal/experimental/consumable_capacity.go#L27-L41, I am not certain whether the code handles names that lack a domain. It directly uses a name from one place (`capacity`) to look up capacity in another (`deviceRequestCapacity.Requests`) without considering that one name might be with and the other without domain.

@sunya-ch, @SchSeba: can you check what happens if a user really drops the domain part?

